$NetBSD$

NetBSD/litex68k: LiteX-68K
==========================

LiteX-68K is a LiteX SoC on an FPGA board (the QMTech Wukong V3) around one
of two soft-cores:
	RD68031		an MC68030 with its MMU and caches, optionally with
			RD68884 (an MC68881)
	RD68041		an MC68040 (with its FPU) or an MC68LC040 (without)
This port is derived from virt68k and runs the stock NetBSD/m68k userland
(MACHINE_ARCH=m68k).  One GENERIC kernel runs on both CPUs.

Booting
-------

The LiteX BIOS loads the images and starts litexboot (litex-68k's
sw/litexboot), which loads the kernel's PT_LOAD segments at the RAM base
(0x4000.0000) + p_paddr, writes Linux/m68k-style bootinfo records right
after the kernel, and jumps to it with the MMU and caches off.  litexboot is
used as is.

The kernel is linked at VA 0x2000, like virt68k, and runs at
VA = PA - firstpa (the classic m68k pmap layout).  locore computes firstpa
from where it was loaded, turns the MMU on with a bootstrap map (VA 0-256MB
onto RAM by a level A table, RAM VA==PA by TT1, the CSRs by TT0) so that C
code runs before the page tables exist, parses the bootinfo, builds the page
tables, and switches to them (TT1 off).  The MC68030 and MC68040 take
different paths there: their MMU, transparent translation and cache control
registers differ.  The CSRs (0xf000.0000 and up) stay mapped VA==PA by TT0,
and the kernel's VA space stops below them.

Devices
-------

From the bootinfo (BI_LITEX_*):
	intr.c		the CPU's interrupt controller (pending/mask CSRs);
			every line on IPL 2, autovectored
	litexctrl	SoC controller: reset
	litextimer	Timer0: hardclock, delay(), 64-bit uptime timecounter
	liteuart	UART: console and tty (ttyLX0)

From the device tree litexboot passes (BI_LITEX_DTB):
	litesdcard	LiteSDCard, an sdmmc(4) host: ld(4)
	liteeth		LiteEth MAC, MDIO bit-banged: rgephy(4)

FPU: BI_FPUTYPE says which FPU there is.  The MC68040's FPU needs FPSP for
the instructions it leaves to software; without an FPU (RD68031 alone, or
the MC68LC040), FPU_EMULATE emulates the MC68881.

Thread pointer
--------------

NetBSD/m68k has no user-readable thread register: every __thread access,
and so most malloc()/free() calls, is an _lwp_getprivate system call.  With
options LITEX68K_FAST_GETPRIVATE (in GENERIC), locore's trap #0 handler
returns it directly, about 4 times faster; traced (ktrace) and non-native
processes still go through syscall().

Clean room
----------

The drivers are written from litex-68k's doc/porting-bsd.md, LiteX's own
BSD-licensed sources (gateware, BIOS libraries, generated csr.h) and the
Motorola manuals.  No GPL code (Linux, QEMU) was used.

Testing
-------

	./build.sh -m litex68k tools kernel=GENERIC release
	litex-68k/scripts/board.py --bitstream DIR boot --kernel netbsd \
	    [--initrd ramdisk.fs] [--cmdline root=ld0a]
	litex-68k/scripts/board.py --bitstream DIR sdboot

LITEX68K_MIN is a smaller kernel, quicker to upload over the serial line.
board.py boot uploads the kernel through the serial line; sdboot starts the
kernel and parameters (params.bin, with the kernel command line) from the
SD card's first partition, a FAT file system (ld0e under NetBSD).

The programs in test/ are built with the cross compiler and run on the
board:
	fptest		FPU context switching: processes (and signal
			handlers) preempting each other mid-workload
	fpstress	each FPU operation, compared with its first pass
			(run two at once)
	fpround		an FPU operand straddling a faulting page, with
			signal handlers, yields and FPCR changes
	tlsbench	system call, thread pointer and malloc()/free() costs
